<file path="README.md">
# ⛪ Church Translator

A live sermon translation web app — the preacher speaks in English and Ukrainian listeners see real-time captions on their phones.

Built with Next.js 16, the Vercel AI SDK, and Claude Haiku.

**Live demo:** [translator-tau-nine.vercel.app](https://translator-tau-nine.vercel.app)

---

## How It Works

1. The **preacher** opens the app, shows the QR code to the congregation, and taps **Start Sermon**
2. The browser's Web Speech API captures speech continuously
3. Each completed sentence is sent to an AI translation API (Claude Haiku)
4. The Ukrainian translation is pushed instantly to all connected listeners via **Server-Sent Events (SSE)**
5. **Listeners** scan the QR code, open the `/listen` page, and read captions in Ukrainian as the sermon unfolds

No app install required — it runs entirely in the browser.

---

## Tech Stack

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Framework | Next.js 16 (App Router) |
| Language | TypeScript |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS v4 |
| AI Translation | Vercel AI SDK + Anthropic Claude Haiku |
| Speech Recognition | Web Speech API (browser-native) |
| Real-time delivery | Server-Sent Events (SSE) |
| QR Code | `qrcode.react` |
| Screen wake lock | Wake Lock API (keeps phone screen on) |

---

## Getting Started

### 1. Clone and install

```bash
git clone <your-repo-url>
cd church-translator
npm install
```

### 2. Set up your API key

Create a `.env.local` file in the project root:

```
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
```

Get a key at [console.anthropic.com](https://console.anthropic.com).

### 3. Run the dev server

```bash
npm run dev
```

Open [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000).

---

## Usage

### Preacher flow
1. Go to `/broadcast`
2. Show the QR code to the congregation (it links them to your local IP)
3. Tap **Start Sermon** and speak naturally in English
4. The app listens, translates each sentence, and broadcasts it automatically

### Listener flow
1. Scan the QR code from the preacher's screen (or navigate to `/listen`)
2. Captions appear automatically as the preacher speaks
3. The latest sentence is displayed prominently; previous lines fade above it

---

## Architecture Notes

- **Translation** — `POST /api/translate` receives a sentence, calls Claude Haiku, and returns the Ukrainian text. It also pushes the result to all SSE subscribers.
- **Streaming** — `GET /api/stream` keeps a long-lived SSE connection open per listener. An in-memory pub/sub store (`lib/broadcast-store.ts`) fans out messages.
- **QR Code** — `GET /api/network-info` returns the server's local IP so the QR code points to the correct address on the local network.
- **Wake Lock** — Both the preacher and listener pages request a screen wake lock so phones don't sleep mid-service.

> **Note:** The in-memory broadcast store works perfectly for a single-server setup (local network use). For a cloud deployment with multiple instances, replace it with a Redis pub/sub layer (e.g. Upstash).

---

## Project Structure

```
app/
  page.tsx              # Home — role selection (Preacher / Listener)
  broadcast/page.tsx    # Preacher UI — speech recognition + QR code
  listen/page.tsx       # Listener UI — real-time Ukrainian captions
  api/
    translate/route.ts  # AI translation endpoint
    stream/route.ts     # SSE endpoint for listeners
    network-info/route.ts # Returns local IP for QR code
lib/
  broadcast-store.ts    # In-memory pub/sub for SSE fan-out
```

---

## Deployment

The app is deployed on [Vercel](https://vercel.com):

**Production URL:** [translator-tau-nine.vercel.app](https://translator-tau-nine.vercel.app)

### Deploy your own

1. Fork this repo
2. Import the project on [vercel.com/new](https://vercel.com/new)
3. Add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` as an environment variable in the Vercel dashboard
4. Deploy — Vercel auto-detects Next.js and handles the rest

### Local network mode

For live use during a church service, run the app locally on a laptop connected to the same Wi-Fi as the congregation:

```bash
npm run dev
```

The preacher's screen shows a QR code pointing to the local IP so attendees can connect directly.

> **Note:** The SSE broadcast store is in-memory, which works perfectly for a single-server setup. On serverless platforms, the preacher and listeners may hit different function instances. For a scalable cloud deployment, swap `lib/broadcast-store.ts` for an Upstash Redis pub/sub adapter.
</file>

